---

import CaseProgressNav from "./CaseProgressNav.astro";

const {
  title = "From Scrolling to Watching",
  year = "2026",
  type = "UX Research",
  role = "Research · Synthesis · Visual Communication",
  figjamUrl = "#",
} = Astro.props;


const methods = [
  {
    number: "01",
    title: "Survey",
    text:
      "The survey gave us a broader view of recurring behaviours, preferences and friction during content discovery.",

    insights: [
      {
        number: "01",
        title: "Discovery often starts elsewhere",
        text:
          "Social media platforms play an important role in discovering what to watch. The journey often begins earlier than users even open a streaming platform.",
      },

      {
        number: "02",
        title: "People want help narrowing the choice",
        text:
          "Participants value their recommendations when they feel relevant to their interests, rather than simply presenting more content. it reduces the effort, which may be very important for the user in the moment.",
      },

      {
        number: "03",
        title: "More choice can create more friction",
        text:
          "Huge variety of content do not automatically make choosing easier. Too many options can extend the browsing process and make choice more difficult.",
      },
    ],
  },

  {
    number: "02",
    title: "Interviews",
    text:
      "Interviews helped us move from what people do toward understanding why they do it.",

    insights: [
      {
        number: "01",
        title: "The right choice depends on the moment",
        text:
          "What people want to watch often changes with mood, energy and situation around the user. A technically \"right\" recommendation is not always contextually relevant.",
      },

      {
        number: "02",
        title: "Value influences the decision",
        text:
          "Availability, subscriptions and the perceived value of a piece of content influence whether people want to continue searching, switch services or abandon a choice.",
      },

      {
        number: "03",
        title: "Recommendations are a shortcut",
        text:
          "People rely on platform recommendations and familiar discovery patterns to reduce the work and effort of searching, especially when they do not already know what they exactly want.",
      },
    ],
  },

  {
    number: "03",
    title: "Cultural Probe",
    text:
      "The cultural probe brought the research closer to the real moment of choice and revealed behaviours that are difficult to reconstruct afterward.",

    insights: [
      {
        number: "01",
        title: "Choosing is often social",
        text:
          "Friends, family and people around the user do influence what gets considered and what eventually gets selected.",
      },

      {
        number: "02",
        title: "Searching has an emotional cost",
        text:
          "Long browsing sessions can change from curiosity into frustration, especially when nothing immediately feels \"right\".",
      },

      {
        number: "03",
        title: "Context changes priorities",
        text:
          "Time, mood, company and the situation around the viewer can matter as much as genre or evenpersonal preferences.",
      },

      {
        number: "04",
        title: "People change strategy when stuck",
        text:
          "When browsing stops working, people move between recommendations, search, external platforms or familiar content instead of following one fixed discovery path.",
      },

      {
        number: "05",
        title: "Decision fatigue develops over time",
        text:
          "The longer users remain undecided, the more effort the process requires — sometimes makes a familiar or easy option more attractive than continueing exploration.",
      },
    ],
  },
];


const processSteps = [
  "Problem",
  "Research Question",
  "Audience",
  "Assumptions",
  "Research",
  "Synthesis",
  "Insight",
];
---


<article class="case-study">

  <a
    class="back-home"
    href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL}
    aria-label="Back to main page"
  >
    <span aria-hidden="true">←</span>
    <span>Back</span>
  </a>

  <!-- ============================================
       HERO
  ============================================= -->

  <header class="case-hero">
    <div class="case-hero__meta">
      <span>{type}</span>
      <span>{year}</span>
    </div>

    <h1>{title}</h1>

    <p class="case-hero__lead">
      What makes someone stop searching and actually press play?
    </p>

    <p class="case-hero__intro">
      A mixed-method UX research project investigating how people
      discover content, make viewing decisions, and experience the
      space between endless browsing and finally choosing something.
    </p>

    <div class="case-hero__details">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Role</span>
        <p>{role}</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Methods</span>
        <p>
          Secondary Research · Survey · Interviews · Cultural Probe
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Replace with hero video / GIF -->
    <!-- <div class="media-placeholder media-placeholder--hero">
      <span>
         <video
  class="case-video"
  src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/research-hero.mp4`}
  autoplay
  muted
  loop
  playsinline
></video> 
      </span>
    </div> -->
  </header>


  <!-- ============================================
       QUICK / FULL TOGGLE
  ============================================= -->

  <nav class="reading-mode" aria-label="Case study reading mode">
    <button
      class="reading-mode__button is-active"
      data-mode="quick"
      type="button"
    >
      Quick read
    </button>

    <button
      class="reading-mode__button"
      data-mode="full"
      type="button"
    >
      Full process
    </button>
  </nav>


  <!-- ============================================
       PROCESS INDEX
  ============================================= -->

  <div class="process-index">
    {processSteps.map((step, index) => (
      <a href={`#section-${index + 1}`}>
        <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
        {step}
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>
 <CaseProgressNav />


  <!-- ============================================
       01 — PROBLEM
  ============================================= -->

  <section
    class="case-section case-section--intro"
    id="section-1"
    data-progress-section
    data-progress-label="Problem"
  >
    <div class="section-number">01</div>

    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">The tension</span>

      <h2>
        Choosing something to watch should be easy.
        It often isn't.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="large-copy">
        Searching for content can become an activity of its own.
        People move between recommendations, categories, platforms
        and short-form feeds before ever committing to something.
      </p>

      <p>
        We, a team of researchers, wanted
        to understand what happens during that decision: what people
        look for, what makes them hesitate, what they trust, and what
        eventually makes them stop scrolling. We wanted to build a DNA of the content-discovery experience, with all its details.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- <div class="media-placeholder"> -->
      <video
  class="case-video"
  src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/research-hero.mp4`}
  autoplay
  muted
  loop
  playsinline
></video> 
    <!-- </div> -->
  </section>


  <!-- ============================================
       02 — RESEARCH QUESTION
       Full-process section
  ============================================= -->

  <section
    class="case-section full-process"
    id="section-2"
    data-progress-section
    data-progress-label="Research question"
  >
    <div class="section-number">02</div>

    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">Framing</span>

      <h2>
        Before researching the answer,
        we had to find the right question.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="large-copy">
        Our initial problem was broad: how might we improve the
        experience of searching for content and what are the triggers that lead to a decision?
      </p>

      <p>
        We decomposed the problem using an Ishikawa diagram and an
        issue tree, separating recommendation, interface, habits,
        context, trust and decision-making into questions we could
        actually investigate.
      </p>
    </div>


    <!-- Storytelling stack -->
    <div class="artifact-story">
      <article class="artifact">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">01</span>
          <h3>Ishikawa diagram</h3>

          <p>
            Breaking one broad problem into possible causes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="media-frame">
        <span class="drag-hint drag-hint--overlay" aria-label="This image can be dragged and zoomed" title="Drag / zoom image">
          <img class="drag-hint__icon" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}icons/drag-black.svg`} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        </span>
        <div
          class="media-placeholder media-pan"
          data-drag-scroll
          aria-label="Ishikawa diagram. Drag horizontally to explore the full image."
        >
          <div class="media-pan__canvas">
            <img
              class="media-pan__image img-ishikawa"
              src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/ishikawa.png`}
              alt="Ishikawa diagram used to break the broad content-discovery problem into possible causes"
              loading="lazy"
              draggable="false"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>
      </article>


      <article class="artifact">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">02</span>
          <h3>Issue tree</h3>

          <p>
            Turning those causes into researchable branches.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="media-frame">
        <span class="drag-hint drag-hint--overlay" aria-label="This image can be dragged and zoomed" title="Drag / zoom image">
          <img class="drag-hint__icon" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}icons/drag-black.svg`} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        </span>
        <div
          class="media-placeholder media-pan"
          data-drag-scroll
          aria-label="Issue tree. Drag horizontally to explore the full image."
        >
          <div class="media-pan__canvas">
            <img
              class="media-pan__image img-issue-tree"
              src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/issue-tree.png`}
              alt="Issue tree turning possible causes into researchable branches"
              loading="lazy"
              draggable="false"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>
      </article>


      <article class="artifact">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">03</span>
          <h3>Refined question</h3>

          <p>
            Narrowing the scope toward the decision behind content
            discovery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="media-frame">
        <span class="drag-hint drag-hint--overlay" aria-label="This image can be dragged and zoomed" title="Drag / zoom image">
          <img class="drag-hint__icon" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}icons/drag-black.svg`} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        </span>
        <div
          class="media-placeholder media-pan"
          data-drag-scroll
          aria-label="Refined research question. Drag horizontally to explore the full image."
        >
          <div class="media-pan__canvas">
            <img
              class="media-pan__image img-research-question"
              src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/research-question.png`}
              alt="Refined research question artefact"
              loading="lazy"
              draggable="false"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ============================================
       03 — AUDIENCE
  ============================================= -->

  <section
    class="case-section full-process"
    id="section-3"
    data-progress-section
    data-progress-label="Audience"
  >
    <div class="section-number">03</div>

    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">Audience</span>

      <h2>
        One audience.
        Different relationships with content.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p>
        We didn't want to treat viewers as one homogeneous group.
        Different levels of familiarity, confidence and engagement
        influence the way people search for and evaluate content.
      </p>
    </div>


    <div class="audience-grid">
      <article>
        <span class="eyebrow">Extreme 01</span>
        <h3>Noob</h3>

        <p>
          The Noob lets the feed decide. One or two devices, a few
          bloggers, free content and the platforms everyone already uses.
          Whatever is trending is usually what ends up on the screen.
        </p>

        <div class="media-frame">
        <span class="drag-hint drag-hint--overlay" aria-label="This image can be dragged and zoomed" title="Drag / zoom image">
          <img class="drag-hint__icon" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}icons/drag-black.svg`} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        </span>
        <div
          class="media-placeholder media-pan"
          data-drag-scroll
          aria-label="Noob archetype visual. Drag horizontally to explore the full image."
        >
          <div class="media-pan__canvas">
            <img
              class="media-pan__image img-noob"
              src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/noob.png`}
              alt="Noob audience archetype visual"
              loading="lazy"
              draggable="false"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>
      </article>


      <article>
        <span class="eyebrow">Extreme 02</span>
        <h3>Nerd</h3>

        <p>
          The Nerd is always searching. Multiple devices, many bloggers,
          ad blockers and new platforms to try. Hours online every day,
          and often the first signs of digital fatigue.
        </p>

        <div class="media-frame">
        <span class="drag-hint drag-hint--overlay" aria-label="This image can be dragged and zoomed" title="Drag / zoom image">
          <img class="drag-hint__icon" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}icons/drag-black.svg`} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        </span>
        <div
          class="media-placeholder media-pan"
          data-drag-scroll
          aria-label="Nerd archetype visual. Drag horizontally to explore the full image."
        >
          <div class="media-pan__canvas">
            <img
              class="media-pan__image img-nerd"
              src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/nerd.png`}
              alt="Nerd audience archetype visual"
              loading="lazy"
              draggable="false"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ============================================
       04 — ASSUMPTIONS / ECOSYSTEM
  ============================================= -->

  <section
    class="case-section full-process"
    id="section-4"
    data-progress-section
    data-progress-label="Assumptions"
  >
    <div class="section-number">04</div>

    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">Scoping</span>

      <h2>
        Separating evidence
        from assumptions.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p>
        Before collecting new data, we mapped what we already knew,
        what we believed to be true, and what still needed evidence.
      </p>
    </div>


    <div class="artifact-story">
      <article class="artifact artifact--reverse">
        <div class="media-frame">
        <span class="drag-hint drag-hint--overlay" aria-label="This image can be dragged and zoomed" title="Drag / zoom image">
          <img class="drag-hint__icon" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}icons/drag-black.svg`} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        </span>
        <div
          class="media-placeholder media-pan"
          data-drag-scroll
          aria-label="Ecosystem map. Drag horizontally to explore the full image."
        >
          <div class="media-pan__canvas">
            <img
              class="media-pan__image img-ecosystem-map"
              src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/ecosystem-map.png`}
              alt="Ecosystem map of the content-discovery space"
              loading="lazy"
              draggable="false"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">01</span>
          <h3>Ecosystem map</h3>

          <p>
            Mapping the people, platforms and systems that shape content
            discovery — and how influence moves between them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>


      <article class="artifact artifact--reverse">
        <div class="media-frame">
        <span class="drag-hint drag-hint--overlay" aria-label="This image can be dragged and zoomed" title="Drag / zoom image">
          <img class="drag-hint__icon" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}icons/drag-black.svg`} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        </span>
        <div
          class="media-placeholder media-pan"
          data-drag-scroll
          aria-label="Assumption matrix. Drag horizontally to explore the full image."
        >
          <div class="media-pan__canvas">
            <img
              class="media-pan__image img-assumption-matrix"
              src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/assumption-matrix.png`}
              alt="Assumption matrix separating what was known from what still needed evidence"
              loading="lazy"
              draggable="false"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">02</span>
          <h3>Assumption matrix</h3>

          <p>
            Prioritising what we believed by impact and uncertainty, so the
            riskiest assumptions could become research priorities.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>


      <article class="artifact artifact--reverse">
        <div class="media-frame">
        <span class="drag-hint drag-hint--overlay" aria-label="This image can be dragged and zoomed" title="Drag / zoom image">
          <img class="drag-hint__icon" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}icons/drag-black.svg`} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        </span>
        <div
          class="media-placeholder media-pan"
          data-drag-scroll
          aria-label="Stakeholder matrix. Drag horizontally to explore the full image."
        >
          <div class="media-pan__canvas">
            <img
              class="media-pan__image img-stakeholders"
              src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/stakeholders.png`}
              alt="Stakeholder matrix for the content-discovery research"
              loading="lazy"
              draggable="false"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">03</span>
          <h3>Stakeholder matrix</h3>

          <p>
            Identifying who affects the discovery experience, what they need,
            and where their interests overlap or pull in different directions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ============================================
       RESEARCH PLAN
  ============================================= -->

  <section class="case-section full-process research-questions-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">Research questions</span>

      <h2>
        Questions that challenged
        what we thought we knew.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="large-copy">
        The research questions became the backbone of the study.
      </p>

      <p>
        Rather than treating our assumptions as facts, we turned them into a
        focused set of questions about discovery, choice, trust and context.
        These questions kept the survey, interviews and cultural probe tied to
        what we actually needed to learn.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="media-frame">
        <span class="drag-hint drag-hint--overlay" aria-label="This image can be dragged and zoomed" title="Drag / zoom image">
          <img class="drag-hint__icon" src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}icons/drag-black.svg`} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        </span>
        <div
      class="media-placeholder media-pan research-questions-media"
      data-drag-scroll
      aria-label="Research questions. Drag horizontally to explore the full image."
    >
      <div class="media-pan__canvas">
        <img
          class="media-pan__image img-research-questions"
          src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}research-case-study/research-questions.png`}
          alt="Group of research questions that guided the study"
          loading="lazy"
          draggable="false"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
        </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ============================================
       05 — METHODS
  ============================================= -->

  <section
    class="case-section"
    id="section-5"
    data-progress-section
    data-progress-label="Research"
  >
    <div class="section-number">05</div>

    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">Primary research</span>

      <h2>
        Three methods.
        Three different views of the same problem.
      </h2>
    </div>


    <div class="methods">
      <div class="method-tabs" role="tablist">
        {methods.map((method, index) => (
          <button
            type="button"
            class:list={[
              "method-tab",
              index === 0 && "is-active",
            ]}
            data-method={index}
          >
            <span>{method.number}</span>
            {method.label}
          </button>
        ))}
      </div>


      <div class="method-panels">
  {methods.map((method, index) => (
    <article
      class:list={[
        "method-panel",
        index === 0 && "is-active",
      ]}
      data-method-panel={index}
    >
      <div class="method-intro">
        <span class="eyebrow">{method.label}</span>

        <h3>{method.title}</h3>

        <p>{method.text}</p>
      </div>

<div class="insights-carousel-wrap">
      <div
        class="insights-carousel"
        aria-label={`${method.label} insights`}
      >
        {method.insights.map((insight) => (
          <article class="insight-card">
            <div class="insight-card__top">
              <span class="insight-card__label">
                Insight
              </span>

              <span class="insight-card__number">
                {insight.number}
              </span>
            </div>

            <div class="insight-card__content">
              <h4>{insight.title}</h4>

              <p>{insight.text}</p>
            </div>

            <span
              class="insight-card__method"
              aria-hidden="true"
            >
              {method.label}
            </span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  ))}
</div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ============================================
       06 — SYNTHESIS
  ============================================= -->

  <section
    class="case-section"
    id="section-6"
    data-progress-section
    data-progress-label="Synthesis"
  >
    <div class="section-number">06</div>

    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="eyebrow">Synthesis</span>

      <h2>
        Turning evidence
        into patterns.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p class="large-copy">
        Collecting information wasn't the difficult part.
        Deciding what it meant together was.
      </p>

      <p>
        Survey responses, interviews and cultural-probe material
        were compared through thematic and segmentation work.
        We looked for recurring patterns — and for contradictions.
      </p>
    </div>


    <div class="synthesis-flow">
      <div class="synthesis-source">
        <span>Survey</span>
        <span>Interviews</span>
        <span>Cultural Probe</span>
      </div>

      <div class="synthesis-arrow">
        ↓
      </div>

      <div class="synthesis-middle">
        <span>Thematic analysis</span>
        <span>Segmentation</span>
        <span>Patterns</span>
      </div>

      <div class="synthesis-arrow">
        ↓
      </div>

      <div class="synthesis-result">
        Insight
      </div>
    </div>


  
  </section>


  <!-- ============================================
       07 — MAIN INSIGHT
  ============================================= -->

  <section
  class="main-insight"
  id="section-7"
  data-progress-section
  data-progress-label="Main insight"
>
  <span class="eyebrow">Our main insight</span>

  <h2>
    People don't want more choice.
    They want more control.
  </h2>

  <p>
    Across the Cultural Probe, users consistently gravitated toward
    experiences that felt familiar, simple and emotionally aligned.
    Overwhelming variety, trend-driven content and algorithmic
    recommendations often created friction instead of confidence.
    The easier it was to maintain a sense of control and emotional
    safety, the easier it became to decide what to watch.
  </p>
</section>


  <!-- ============================================
       OUTCOME
  ============================================= -->

  <section class="case-section outcome-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">Outcome</span>

    <h2>
      The outcome was a direction,
      not an interface.
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="section-body outcome-copy">
    <p>
      The project produced a clearer understanding of the content-discovery
      problem rather than a finished interface. Through secondary research,
      surveys, interviews and a cultural probe, we were able to define the
      audience more precisely, challenge our initial assumptions and identify
      recurring patterns across different research methods.
    </p>

    <p>
      The strongest direction that emerged was the importance of control,
      familiarity and emotional alignment during content discovery. Instead
      of simply giving users more recommendations or more choice, future
      concepts should help reduce cognitive effort and make the decision
      process feel calmer, more predictable and more personal.
    </p>

    <p>
      This research therefore became a foundation for future design work:
      a clearer problem space, an evidence-backed design direction and a set
      of principles that could be translated into concepts and prototypes
      in the next phase.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>


  


  <!-- ============================================
       FIGJAM
  ============================================= -->

  <section class="project-link-block project-link-block--figjam">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">Full process</span>

